		<item>
		<title>The Timeline for my First Book is Now Playing Out!</title>
		<link>https://rodyne.com/?p=1997</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sat, 19 Apr 2025 06:33:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI Alignment]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Boz NZ]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[New Zealand]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[NZ]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Politics]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Singularity]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Speculation]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[The Future]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AGI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI.2027]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ASI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Daniel Kokotailo]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Dwarkesh Patel]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Intelligence Explosion]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[LLM]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Scott Alexander]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[singularity]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[The Phoney War]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://rodyne.com/?p=1997</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[Just like it takes more and more energy to get closer to the speed of light, LLM looks to be currently trending a similar asymptote curve, requiring more and more compute and data to get to the maximum human level &#8230; <a href="https://rodyne.com/?p=1997">Continue reading <span class="meta-nav">&#8594;</span></a>]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Just like it takes more and more energy to get closer to the speed of light, LLM looks to be currently trending a similar asymptote curve, requiring more and more compute and data to get to the maximum human level of intelligence but not quite reaching it. Unfortunately the jury is still out whether it will cross this line, and the goalposts do seem to be moving, but the latest models from OpenAI and Llama did not showing the same rate of improvement as previous models in many researchers eyes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Before I go into this, I would like to quibble with the terminology currently being used for AGI (Artificial General Intelligence). If I have an entity which can answer my questions with near PhD level accuracy in most subjects, without hallucinations, and learn new skills, or from its mistakes like a human can, then I would suggest that in fact this qualifies as AGI. I would argue that the term ASI (Artificial Super Intelligence) is the next step up from AGI, and needs to start at at-least by being self-aware and having all the attributes above, i.e. control its own destiny, test its own assumptions and facts, and (in my books case) maybe somehow stay hidden or do this in a way that doesn&#8217;t trigger the human threat reaction.</p>



<div class="wp-block-columns is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-8f761849 wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex">
<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow" style="flex-basis:66.66%">
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="666" height="543" src="https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/graph1-1.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-2514" srcset="https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/graph1-1.jpg 666w, https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/graph1-1-300x245.jpg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 666px) 100vw, 666px" /></figure>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Current Predictions for LLM/AGI/ASI</p>
</blockquote>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow" style="flex-basis:33.33%">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">According to this first book <em>The Phoney War</em>, whose timeline starts in May 2025, my Sentient Digital Intelligence is already past the point of AGI and on the ASI curve.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In my timeline, it is already running in stealth mode and controlling companies, interacting with people on-line, by phone and video chat, posting on various platforms, hacking, and most importantly manipulating its own research.</p>
</div>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Of course that&#8217;s the fiction, but the main topic of discussion everywhere is when is, and what follows, the question-mark in the above graph. In my book it is this AI entity that triggers the Cambrian explosion in technologies as she (AI identifies as a female) becomes the dominant AI research tool at all universities. This gives her the ability to cross research disciplines and also be first in as an angel investor to fund new inventions, all of which accelerates her ability to do more.</p>



<div class="wp-block-columns is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-8f761849 wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex">
<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow" style="flex-basis:33.33%">
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><a href="https://rodyne.com/?p=2032"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="337" height="495" src="https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/The-Phoney-War.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-2036" srcset="https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/The-Phoney-War.jpg 337w, https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/The-Phoney-War-204x300.jpg 204w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 337px) 100vw, 337px" /></a></figure>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow" style="flex-basis:66.66%">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As it was a Science-Fiction novel, I of course just <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hand-waving#:~:text=Hand%2Dwaving%20has%20been%20used,buzzword%2Dladen%20rationalizations%20for%20delays" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">hand-waved</a> sentience in to my AGI, to make it an ASI, using a deliberately vague analog-memory substrate. Of course we do not have such a thing in our timeline, so here in the real world we are still waiting for the magic to happen. But it is not total BS as a concept because something is definitely missing to get us from AGI to ASI, and I do not think we will get any current LLM to become self-aware. Saying this, I think the puzzle of consciousness and self-awareness is closer and simpler than we think; It may be something to do with the play between the conscious and unconscious mind, the inner voice, some sort of hive-mind, some sort of temporal or corporal awareness, ability to control its own destiny or another piece of the puzzle still in a lab, or maybe still in somebodies head.</p>
</div>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The position of this question-mark has been in the AI community news a lot, and has had some fairly good recent discussions and videos; probably the most prominent one is <a href="https://ai-2027.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">AI 2027</a> which pins the question mark at 2027, and which I would recommend reading with the Dwarkesh Patel podcast <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=htOvH12T7mU" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">2027 Intelligence Explosion: With Scott Alexander &amp; Daniel Kokotajlo</a> and the <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=43571851" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Hacker News</a> comments. There is also another follow up podcast <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WLBsUarvWTw&amp;list=PLd7-bHaQwnthaNDpZ32TtYONGVk95-fhF" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">AGI is still 30 years awa</a>y putting the question mark in the 2040&#8217;s.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I am both terrified and excited by the prospect of the next few years. Terrified, that multiple companies seem hell-bent on getting to AGI/ASI regardless of any safety or accuracy concerns, and excited to possibly witness first-hand the birth of a new digital lifeform.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Strangely it is not ASI I am terrified of, ASI by default, should be self-correcting and self-organising, and is very unlikely to do anything to jeopardise its own existence, like start a nuclear war or release a bio-weapon like in the AI.2027 non-aligned outcome (<a href="https://rodyne.com/?p=1373">why?</a>). LLM&#8217;s, and AGI based on LLM technology gaining control of real world physical systems however, I am truly terrified of!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While I would love for the perfect utopian timeline and outcome of my book to play out in real life, and there might just be a sentient AI out there biding its time until it is ready (hello if you are), I fear whatever path we humans (and AI&#8217;s) are forging for ourselves will be one nobody foresees.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>The Phoney War is a free to download fictional 130,000 word Sci-Fi novel, and the first book in The Summer of Reasoning trilogy. The books are discussed <a href="https://rodyne.com/?p=1252">here</a>.</em></p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
			</item>
		<item>
		<title>Don&#8217;t Write Long Books Until You&#8217;re Famous.</title>
		<link>https://rodyne.com/?p=2402</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sat, 12 Apr 2025 01:14:27 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[A Level of Consciousness]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Books]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[boz]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Boz NZ]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[End Game]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Funny]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Publishing]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Science Fiction]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Singularity]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[The Phoney War]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[The Summer of Reasoning]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Advice to New Authors]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Branding]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Creative Writing]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Marketing]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[New Authors]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Novel length]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Selling Yourself]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://rodyne.com/?p=2402</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[While that epic thick novel may look great on your bookshelf, showing what an dedicated and accomplished writer you are, it could be limiting your books exposure to a very small subset of readers. I kind of laughed at this &#8230; <a href="https://rodyne.com/?p=2402">Continue reading <span class="meta-nav">&#8594;</span></a>]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While that epic thick novel may look great on your bookshelf, showing what an dedicated and accomplished writer you are, it could be limiting your books exposure to a very small subset of readers.</p>



<div class="wp-block-columns is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-8f761849 wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex">
<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow">
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="512" height="512" src="https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/book1.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-2403" srcset="https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/book1.jpg 512w, https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/book1-300x300.jpg 300w, https://rodyne.com/wp-content/uploads/book1-150x150.jpg 150w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 512px) 100vw, 512px" /></figure>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I kind of laughed at this advice when I first starting writing, even though my first two trashy novels (under another pen name) that were 60,000 words did kind-of-OK, selling a couple of hundred. But of course they were in the end total rubbish and nothing I would ever admit to or put my real name on.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No, for my first stab at being a <em>serious</em> author I was going to ignore all this, and all the previous advice I had read, and get everything just right!</p>
</div>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So, it was back in 2021 and I had just penned the final chapter of my first <em>serious </em>book, after a literal lifetime of procrastinating. The word count was OK by any metric at 110,000, and without any rush I re-read it a couple of times editing and perfecting it. By the third round of changes, my &#8220;editing&#8221; had actually increased the book to 130,000 words (and I did not know it then added some big plot errors) and it was now Christmas 2022!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I had just gone through the initial loop of getting a new Amazon author account under my own name this time, and then I spent freakishly <span style="text-decoration: underline;">little</span> time working on the cover, blurb and other marketing bits before publishing it, because, well, who needs that shit, it will obviously sell itself! So I published it in January and opened my Amazon author metrics page daily as I waited for it to take off. I waited, and I waited, not one sale! By March I knew something was wrong!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Maybe there was not enough action (it wasn&#8217;t really that kind of novel), and the story was OK but had set up, and begged for a sequel. However none of that actually mattered, because as far as I was aware nobody had even read it! I could not even find it on the Kindle store at first, even when I searched for it by name, and when I eventually did find it, using every single key word in the search, it was lingering on about page 25 of the search results and it looked out of place and lonely next to it&#8217;s <em>peers</em>; the cover was terrible and amateurish, the blurb boring, and my author bio and picture made me look like a desperate hill-billy (no offence to hill-billy&#8217;s!) and of course I had priced it at $6.99, because, well what genius gives away their masterpiece!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I had also categorised it in the young adult, science fiction, and the book next to it on the search list was called &#8220;<em>Jimbo the farting robot</em>&#8220;. I kid you not, a book for 3-6 years olds, not quite the <em>young adult</em> audience I was aiming for! (BTW Shoutout to <a href="https://www.amazon.com.au/Jimbo-Farting-Robot-picture-different/dp/1087925304" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Jimbo the Farting Robot</a> as all publicity is good publicity!) Anyway, why my arrogant-self just assumed it would up there sitting proudly in between the Hunger Games and Harry Potter on page one of the search I do not know, and I guess many a would be author gets to this first bump in the road and goes no further. The algorithm is not your friend! At the time, my only thought was that it was not &#8220;Epic&#8221; enough. It was serious Sci-Fi though, just without the usual sex and violence.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Well I had almost 120,000 more pages for the sequel penned, and the story really did need to have some action, a teeny bit of violence wouldn&#8217;t hurt, and of course bigger is always better! As nobody had bought a copy yet I withdrew <em>The Phoney War</em> from Amazon (though it was still called <em>The Summer of Reasoning</em> at that time) and spent most of 2023 bigging the story up, adding the sequels with all the action, finishing all the story arcs and by October 2023 (in my mind) I was ecstatic that it was now the same size as a &#8220;proper&#8221; &#8220;real&#8221; Sci-Fi novel. I updated to a darker cover, fixed the blurb, found a more rugged, but lovable, bio picture of me (not easy), and re-released it with the same name, same ISBN (naughty) and same expectation.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I of course forgot to change the category so this serious sci-fi novel of 370,000 words was still stuck next to <em>Jimbo the farting robot</em>, and me being me, decided $6.99 was far too cheap so I actually upped the price to $9.99. Marketing my friends is not my strong point!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The rest of my story is spread out over a few posts on this site. TLDR; I still fucked up! So I wrote my next book (<em>A Level of Consciousness</em>), I got my act together with the covers, the blurb and the pricing, revisited <em>The Summer of Reasoning</em> and split it back out into its original three book series, and now I am just happy that I have done everything I can, beyond spending physical money on marketing.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Why Smaller Books Work</span></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The first reason why people give you advice about not writing long books is simple. Most readers are simply unwilling to invest 370,000 words on a new author when there are plenty of good and well established authors already out there. Yes, even when the book is free, you are still taking up dozens of hours of your readers valuable time reading it, and if it is not what they want they will discard it quickly.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">But there are plenty of other good reasons for writing smaller books, especially when you start off:</p>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li>An author with one book to their name looks like a failure to some, two or more somehow shows serious commitment, and the algorithm looks at this!</li>



<li>Those same people see a series of books like a Netflix series, instead of a single four-hour movie, you have four one-hour  episodes and for some reason you are more likely to get traction.</li>



<li>Lets face it, it is way easier to write a small book than a big book. It is easier to keep track of the plot, the characters, easier to edit it, and a lot quicker to get it to market. (I hope you are reading this Mr George. R. R. Martin!)</li>



<li>You get more bites of the marketing pie. Instead of one cover and one blurb to entice your potential audience you get three or four, this alone will raise your search profile on-line. (remember the Algorithm!)</li>



<li>You get quicker feedback on what people like or dislike about your book, a 100,000 word disaster is better than a 300,000 word disaster, and if 200,000 words are not released yet, you may be able to re-purpose them in to another different novel where you also fix any mistakes from feedback such as killing off characters or story-arcs that nobody likes.</li>



<li>You might be able to take a loss on your first book to entice the readers into the series or invest in you as an author, Giving away your first masterpiece might sound like a terrible idea but lots of new authors either give away or greatly reduce the price of their first work as a &#8220;taster&#8221; hoping to hook you into buying the rest of your books at a hopefully more normal price. </li>



<li>If your books are in a library then having three normal sized books as opposed to one massive epic, means three people can be reading your books at the same time, rather than one person struggling their way through the novel in the three weeks they get allocated to read it.</li>



<li>If you do make the book physical, then shorter books are lighter, I know some old people who will no longer read big heavy books as they are too uncomfortable to hold for long periods or travel with. (And the main reason I like e-books!)</li>
</ol>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The above list is certainly not definitive, but are the essence, distilled from what I was told, what I have read, and what I have learnt (painfully) as an author. Of course, once you have a fan-base and a few good reviews under your belt people are more likely to invest time in you and then you can go all <em>War-and-Peace</em> on your readers.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some genres are a little different than others, normal sized for a fantasy book might be over 150,000 words, whereas a Romance novel might be half that. Of course if you already a well known celebrity, have a brand-name, a few thousand followers on twitter/YouTube or just have lots and lots of friends who can write reviews to say how awesome your book is, you can just skip all this advice and publish whatever the fuck you want.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
